# Copy this file to `.env` and fill in real values.
# `.env` is gitignored and must never be committed.

# ---- Database (Railway Postgres) ----
# Railway's Postgres plugin exposes this as DATABASE_URL; either name works.
DATABASE_URI=postgresql://user:password@host:5432/railway

# ---- Payload ----
# A long random string. Generate with: openssl rand -hex 32
PAYLOAD_SECRET=

# No trailing slash. Locally http://localhost:3000,
# on Railway the https://<project>.railway.app URL, later the real domain.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SERVER_URL=http://localhost:3000

# ---- Image storage (Railway Storage Bucket, S3-compatible) ----
# Without these, uploads land on the local filesystem and Railway
# wipes them on every rebuild.
S3_ENDPOINT=
S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=
S3_BUCKET=
S3_REGION=us-east-1

# ---- Enquiry notifications ----
# Without these, an enquiry sent from the website is saved and nobody is told:
# it waits in the admin panel until somebody opens it. With them, it is emailed
# the moment it arrives.
#
# Any SMTP account works — a Gmail address with an app password, or whatever
# the hotels already use. The site starts normally whether or not these are
# set, and never waits on the mail server.
SMTP_HOST=
SMTP_PORT=587
SMTP_USER=
SMTP_PASS=
# Who the mail appears to come from. Defaults to SMTP_USER.
SMTP_FROM=
SMTP_FROM_NAME=My Flower Hotels

# Where enquiries go when a hotel has no email of its own and Settings has
# none either. Set at least this one.
ENQUIRY_NOTIFY_EMAIL=

# ---- Misc ----
# Secret used to authenticate scheduled jobs
CRON_SECRET=
# Used to validate draft-preview requests from the admin panel
PREVIEW_SECRET=

# ---- Google Hotel Center ----
# Where My Flower's prices are pushed so they can appear on the Google listing
# beside Booking.com and Agoda. All three come from a Hotel Center account —
# there is nothing to invent here, and without them the site simply does not
# push (it still serves /google/hotels.xml and /google/rates.xml for Google to
# fetch, once the switch in Site settings is on).
#
# With them set, every price typed into the calendar and every booking taken is
# sent to Google within the second, rather than waiting to be collected.
GOOGLE_ARI_ENDPOINT=
GOOGLE_ARI_USERNAME=
GOOGLE_ARI_PASSWORD=

# ---- Visitor analytics ----
# The Google Analytics measurement ID, which looks like G-XXXXXXXXXX. It is
# made at analytics.google.com: create a property for the site, add a Web data
# stream for its address, and the ID is on the stream's page.
#
# Left empty, the site loads nothing and counts nobody — which is deliberate,
# so that pages opened in development and the hundreds opened by the test suite
# never appear in the figures as guests. Set it only on the live deployment.
#
# This is separate from Search Console, which needs nothing set here: the
# verification is already in the site's own code, and it shows the last sixteen
# months at search.google.com/search-console whether this is set or not.
#
# Note that it sets cookies in the visitor's browser, and European law wants a
# visitor's agreement before that. There is no consent banner on the site yet.
NEXT_PUBLIC_GA_MEASUREMENT_ID=

# Reports a confirmed or cancelled booking to Google from the server, rather
# than trusting the guest's browser to do it. A large share of phones block
# Google's script: without this, those visits are counted and the bookings they
# produced are not, so the site reads as one that cannot sell.
#
# Made in Analytics > Admin > Data streams > the stream > Measurement Protocol
# API secrets > Create. It is a server secret and never reaches the browser,
# which is why it has no NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix.
#
# Useless on its own — NEXT_PUBLIC_GA_MEASUREMENT_ID has to be set too.
GA_API_SECRET=

# ---- Scheduled work ----
# The password for /next/close-stays, which closes out stays that have ended
# and pays the points they earned. Any long random string; unset means the
# endpoint refuses everybody, including a scheduler, rather than running open.
#
# It matters more than it sounds. A booking is created as `confirmed` and
# nothing else in the site ever moves it on, so every stay ever taken is still
# filed as an upcoming arrival — and points are only credited on a `completed`
# one. The site promises guests that booking direct earns points; until this
# runs, not one has ever been paid.
#
# Call it once a day, by either route:
#
#   a) Railway: + Create -> Empty Service, set its start command to
#      curl -fsS -X POST -H "Authorization: Bearer $JOB_SECRET" \
#        https://myflowerhotels.com/next/close-stays
#      and give the service a cron schedule of "0 4 * * *" (04:00 UTC, which is
#      07:00 in Erbil — after every checkout and before anybody looks).
#
#   b) A free scheduler such as cron-job.org: same URL, method POST, with the
#      Authorization header above. Nothing to deploy.
#
# Safe to call as often as you like: it only touches rows it has not already
# changed, and the points routine refuses to pay the same stay twice.
JOB_SECRET=

# --- Telling search engines a page changed -----------------------------------
#
# Any random string of 8 to 128 characters, using only the digits 0-9 and the
# letters a-f. Keep it the same once set. Generate one with:
#
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(16).toString('hex'))"
#
# What it does: every time a hotel or a room is saved in the admin panel, the
# site submits the pages that changed to IndexNow, which Bing, Yandex, Seznam
# and Naver all read. They look within hours instead of within weeks.
#
# Why it is worth setting for a hotel in Erbil, where Bing sends almost no
# traffic: the assistants that search the live web draw a meaningful share of
# their results from Bing's index. A page Bing has not re-read is a page an
# assistant cannot cite, however well it ranks on Google.
#
# The site publishes the key at /indexnow.txt so the search engine can check it
# belongs to this domain. That happens on its own — there is nothing to upload.
# Unset means off: nothing is submitted and nothing is served at that address.
INDEXNOW_KEY=

# --- Card payments -----------------------------------------------------------
#
# All of this is empty until the payment processor hands over credentials, and
# while it is empty the site behaves exactly as it always has: guests book with
# a name and a telephone number and pay at the hotel. Nothing here changes that
# on its own — "Card payments" in Site settings has to be switched on as well.
#
# Give this whole block to the processor's engineer. It is the shape of the
# integration; see also docs/payment-gateway-integration.md.

# Where to ask for a payment, and the key to authenticate with. ①
PAYMENT_API_URL=
PAYMENT_API_KEY=
# Some processors also want a merchant or terminal id in the request.
PAYMENT_MERCHANT_ID=

# What the pay button and the admin panel call this processor.
PAYMENT_PROVIDER_LABEL=

# The shared secret their webhook is signed with, and the header it arrives in.
# Without the secret no callback is believed, which is the intended behaviour:
# an unsigned "this booking is paid" message is a free room for anyone who
# learns the address. ④
PAYMENT_WEBHOOK_SECRET=
PAYMENT_SIGNATURE_HEADER=x-signature

# Where things are in their JSON, when the usual names do not match. Dotted
# paths, e.g. data.checkout.url — set only if the site reports it cannot find
# the redirect. ③
PAYMENT_REDIRECT_PATH=
PAYMENT_REFERENCE_PATH=
PAYMENT_CALLBACK_REFERENCE_PATH=
PAYMENT_CALLBACK_STATUS_PATH=

# Which of their status words mean the money is taken, comma separated. ⑤
# Anything not on this list counts as not paid, deliberately.
PAYMENT_PAID_STATUSES=paid,captured,success,successful,completed,approved,settled
#
# ⑥ THE ONE THAT COSTS REAL MONEY IF IT IS WRONG.
#
# Gateways take an integer in the currency's smallest unit. For dollars that is
# cents and nobody argues. For the Iraqi dinar there is a real disagreement:
# ISO 4217 says a dinar is 1000 fils, so 250,000 IQD would be sent as
# 250000000 — but fils have not circulated for decades and many processors here
# treat IQD as whole units, wanting 250000.
#
# Ask them: "do I send 250,000 IQD as 250000 or as 250000000?"
#   250000    -> set 0
#   250000000 -> set 3
#
# Until this is set the site refuses to start any IQD payment and says why in
# the log. That refusal is on purpose. Guessing high charges a guest a thousand
# times too much on the first live transaction.
PAYMENT_CURRENCY_EXPONENT_IQD=
# Dollars need no answer; 2 everywhere. Set only to override.
PAYMENT_CURRENCY_EXPONENT_USD=
